"Release xxHash v0.8.4 · Cyan4973/xxHash · GitHub (https://github.com/Cyan4973/xxHash/releases/tag/v0.8.4)\nciteturn122view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/Cyan4973/xxHash/releases/tag/v0.8.4\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 264\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCollision ratio comparison · Cyan4973/xxHash Wiki · GitHub (https://github.com/Cyan4973/xxHash/wiki/Collision-ratio-comparison)\nciteturn122view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/Cyan4973/xxHash/wiki/Collision-ratio-comparison\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 243\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPerformance comparison · Cyan4973/xxHash Wiki · GitHub (https://github.com/Cyan4973/xxHash/wiki/Performance-comparison)\nciteturn122view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/Cyan4973/xxHash/wiki/Performance-comparison\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 247\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nxxHash — extremely fast hash algorithm (https://xxhash.com/)\nciteturn122view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://xxhash.com/\",\"lineno\":0}); Total lines: 278\nL0: cite0†xxHash cite1†Speed cite2†Try it cite3†Languages cite4†Used by cite5†Docs cite6†Latest release†github.com cite7†GitHub†github.com L1: \nL2: # xxHash\nL3: \nL4: Hashing faster than RAM speed\nL5: \nL6: A cite8†non-cryptographic†en.wikipedia.org hash algorithm, proposed in four flavors: XXH32, XXH64, XXH3_64bits and XXH3_128bits.\nL7: \nL8: cite9†Get started†github.com cite2†Try it in your browser cite5†API docs cite10†Star†github.com L9: \nL10: cite4†and 44 other projects → L11: ## Choosing a variant\nL12: \nL13: All four are fully supported and specified. They differ in output width and in speed — the cite1†benchmarks show by how much.\nL14: Variant  | Output  | Notes\nL15: --- | --- | ---\nL16: `XXH3_64bits`  | 64-bit  | Fastest, and the strongest on small inputs. The recommended default.\nL17: `XXH3_128bits`  | 128-bit  | Same speed as `XXH3_64bits`, with a wider output.\nL18: `XXH64`  | 64-bit  | Fast on 64-bit platforms, and widely deployed.\nL19: `XXH32`  | 32-bit  | Fastest of the 32-bit hashes, and favoured on 32-bit targets.\nL20: All variants are stable. The same input always produces the same hash, in any version, on any platform. Hashes stored today remain valid tomorrow, as documented by the cite11†format specification†github.com .\nL21: \nL22: Not cryptographic. xxHash is built for speed, not for resisting an adversary. Do not use it for signatures, password storage, or any place where an attacker chooses the input and profits from a collision.\nL23: ## Speed\nL24: \nL25: Single-threaded bandwidth on a 100 KB input, which stays in CPU cache. The memcpy bar is measured from RAM instead — it is there to show where the memory wall sits, not as a like-for-like comparison.\nL26: \nL27: [Button: Intel i7-9700K] [Button: Apple M1 Pro] [Button: AMD Zen 5]\nL28: \nL29: XXH3 (AVX2)\nL30: \nL31: 59.4 GB/s\nL32: \nL33: XXH128 (AVX2)\nL34: \nL35: 57.9 GB/s\nL36: \nL37: XXH3 (SSE2)\nL38: \nL39: 31.5 GB/s\nL40: \nL41: XXH128 (SSE2)\nL42: \nL43: 29.6 GB/s\nL44: \nL45: memcpy, from RAM\nL46: \nL47: 28.0 GB/s\nL48: \nL49: XXH64\nL50: \nL51: 19.4 GB/s\nL52: \nL53: XXH32\nL54: \nL55: 9.7 GB/s\nL56: \nL57: memcpy, from RAM\nL58: \nL59: 45 GB/s\nL60: \nL61: XXH3 (NEON)\nL62: \nL63: 36.3 GB/s\nL64: \nL65: XXH128 (NEON)\nL66: 35.1 GB/s\nL67: \nL68: XXH64\nL69: \nL70: 13.4 GB/s\nL71: \nL72: XXH32\nL73: \nL74: 6.6 GB/s\nL75: \nL76: XXH3 (AVX512)\nL77: \nL78: 159 GB/s\nL79: \nL80: XXH128 (AVX512)\nL81: \nL82: 156 GB/s\nL83: \nL84: XXH3 (AVX2)\nL85: \nL86: 84.0 GB/s\nL87: \nL88: XXH128 (AVX2)\nL89: \nL90: 83.5 GB/s\nL91: \nL92: XXH3 (SSE2)\nL93: \nL94: 38.9 GB/s\nL95: \nL96: XXH128 (SSE2)\nL97: \nL98: 37.4 GB/s\nL99: \nL100: memcpy, from RAM\nL101: \nL102: 28.5 GB/s\nL103: \nL104: XXH64\nL105: \nL106: 27.8 GB/s\nL107: \nL108: XXH32\nL109: \nL110: 13.8 GB/s\nL111: \nL112: Measured with the cite12†open source benchmark program†github.com . On 32-bit targets the ranking shifts considerably in favour of 32-bit hashes — see cite13†the wiki†github.com .\nL113: \nL114: Compare with other hash algorithms\nL115: Intel i7-9700K, Ubuntu x64 20.04, `clang` v10.0 `-O3`. Small data velocity is a rough measure of efficiency on short inputs; see cite14†the wiki†github.com for the detailed analysis.\nL116: Hash  | Width  | Bandwidth  | Small data  | Note\nL117: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL118: XXH3 (AVX2)  | 64  | 59.4 GB/s  | 133.1  | AVX2 support (optional)\nL119: XXH128 (AVX2)  | 128  | 57.9 GB/s  | 118.1  | AVX2 support (optional)\nL120: XXH3 (SSE2)  | 64  | 31.5 GB/s  | 133.1  |\nL121: XXH128 (SSE2)  | 128  | 29.6 GB/s  | 118.1  |\nL122: memcpy  | —  | 28.0 GB/s  | —  | from RAM, for reference\nL123: City64  | 64  | 22.0 GB/s  | 76.6  |\nL124: T1ha2  | 64  | 22.0 GB/s  | 99.0  | Slightly worse cite15†collisions†github.com L125: City128  | 128  | 21.7 GB/s  | 57.7  |\nL126: XXH64  | 64  | 19.4 GB/s  | 71.0  |\nL127: SpookyHash  | 64  | 19.3 GB/s  | 53.2  |\nL128: Mum  | 64  | 18.0 GB/s  | 67.0  | Slightly worse cite15†collisions†github.com L129: XXH32  | 32  | 9.7 GB/s  | 71.9  |\nL130: City32  | 32  | 9.1 GB/s  | 66.0  |\nL131: Murmur3  | 32  | 3.9 GB/s  | 56.1  |\nL132: SipHash  | 64  | 3.0 GB/s  | 43.2  |\nL133: FNV64  | 64  | 1.2 GB/s  | 62.7  | Poor avalanche properties\nL134: Blake2  | 256  | 1.1 GB/s  | 5.1  | Cryptographic\nL135: SHA1  | 160  | 0.8 GB/s  | 5.6  | Cryptographic but broken\nL136: MD5  | 128  | 0.6 GB/s  | 7.8  | Cryptographic but broken\nL137: ## Try it\nL138: \nL139: xxHash compiled to WebAssembly, run