: \nL254: cite108†Netflix†partnerhelp.netflixstudios.com cite109†rsync†rsync.samba.org cite110†LZ4†www.lz4.org cite111†Silverstack†pomfort.com cite112†Rapidcopy†www.lespace.co.jp cite113†Hedge†www.hedgeformac.com cite114†fastcopy†ipmsg.org cite115†TeraCopy†codesector.com cite116†Hammer 2†www.dragonflybsd.org cite117†CHK†compressme.net L255: ### Other\nL256: \nL257: cite118†Visual Studio†devblogs.microsoft.com cite119†NSight compute†docs.nvidia.com cite120†Xpra†www.xpra.org cite121†TeamViewer†www.teamviewer.com cite122†Factor†factorcode.org cite123†nVBio†nvlabs.github.io cite124†Genozip†genozip.com cite125†dvisvgm†dvisvgm.de cite126†FastBuild†www.fastbuild.org cite127†Keypirinha†keypirinha.com cite128†QuickHash†quickhash-gui.org L258: \nL259: #### Documentation\nL260: \nL261:   * cite5†API reference --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nxxHash: xxHash (https://xxhash.com/doc/v0.8.4/index.html)\nciteturn122view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://xxhash.com/doc/v0.8.4/index.html\",\"lineno\":250}); Total lines: 297\nL58: ## Usage\nL59: \nL60: All xxHash variants use a similar API. Changing the algorithm is a trivial substitution.\nL61: \nL62: Precondition\nL63:     For functions which take an input and length parameter, the following requirements are assumed:\nL64: \nL65:   * The range from [`input`, `input + length`) is valid, readable memory.\nL66:     * The only exception is if the `length` is `0`, `input` may be `NULL`.\nL67:   * For C++, the objects must have the TriviallyCopyable property, as the functions access bytes directly as if it was an array of `unsigned char`.\nL68: Single Shot\nL69: \nL70: These functions are stateless functions which hash a contiguous block of memory, immediately returning the result. They are the easiest and usually the fastest option.\nL71: \nL72: cite6†XXH32() , cite5†XXH64() , cite3†XXH3_64bits() , cite4†XXH3_128bits() L73: #include <string.h>\nL74: #include \"cite8†xxhash.h \"\nL75: \nL76: // Example for a function which hashes a null terminated string with XXH32().\nL77: \nL78: cite9†XXH32_hash_t hash_string(const char* string, cite9†XXH32_hash_t seed)\nL79: \nL80: {\nL81: \nL82: // NULL pointers are only valid if the length is zero\nL83: \nL84: size_t length = (string == NULL) ? 0 : strlen(string);\nL85: \nL86: return cite6†XXH32 (string, length, seed);\nL87: \nL88: }\nL89: \nL90: cite6†XXH32 L91: \nL92: XXH32_hash_t XXH32(const void *input, size_t length, XXH32_hash_t seed)\nL93: \nL94: Calculates the 32-bit hash of input using xxHash32.\nL95: \nL96: Definition xxhash.h:3207\nL97: cite9†XXH32_hash_t L98: \nL99: uint32_t XXH32_hash_t\nL100: \nL101: An unsigned 32-bit integer.\nL102: \nL103: Definition xxhash.h:588\nL104: \nL105: cite8†xxhash.h L106: \nL107: Streaming\nL108: \nL109: These groups of functions allow incremental hashing of unknown size, even more than what would fit in a size_t.\nL110: \nL111: cite10†XXH32_reset() , cite11†XXH64_reset() , cite12†XXH3_64bits_reset() , cite13†XXH3_128bits_reset() L112: #include <stdio.h>\nL113: \nL114: #include <assert.h>\nL115: #include \"cite8†xxhash.h \"\nL116: \nL117: // Example for a function which hashes a FILE incrementally with XXH3_64bits().\nL118: \nL119: cite14†XXH64_hash_t hashFile(FILE* f)\nL120: \nL121: {\nL122: \nL123: // Allocate a state struct. Do not just use malloc() or new.\nL124: \nL125: cite15†XXH3_state_t * state = cite16†XXH3_createState ();\nL126: \nL127: assert(state != NULL && \"Out of memory!\");\nL128: \nL129: // Reset the state to start a new hashing session.\nL130: \nL131: cite12†XXH3_64bits_reset (state);\nL132: \nL133: char buffer[4096];\nL134: \nL135: size_t count;\nL136: \nL137: // Read the file in chunks\nL138: while ((count = fread(buffer, 1, sizeof(buffer), f)) != 0) {\nL139: \nL140: // Run update() as many times as necessary to process the data\nL141: \nL142: cite17†XXH3_64bits_update (state, buffer, count);\nL143: \nL144: }\nL145: \nL146: // Retrieve the finalized hash. This will not change the state.\nL147: \nL148: cite14†XXH64_hash_t result = cite18†XXH3_64bits_digest (state);\nL149: \nL150: // Free the state. Do not use free().\nL151: \nL152: cite19†XXH3_freeState (state);\nL153: \nL154: return result;\nL155: \nL156: }\nL157: \nL158: cite18†XXH3_64bits_digest L159: \nL160: XXH64_hash_t XXH3_64bits_digest(const XXH3_state_t *statePtr)\nL161: Returns the calculated XXH3 64-bit hash value from an XXH3_state_t.\nL162: \nL163: Definition xxhash.h:6877\nL164: \nL165: cite16†XXH3_createState L166: \nL167: XXH3_state_t * XXH3_createState(void)\nL168: \nL169: Allocate an XXH3_state_t.\nL170: \nL171: Definition xxhash.h:6575\nL172: \nL173: cite17†XXH3_64bits_update L174: \nL175: XXH_errorcode XXH3_64bits_update(XXH3_state_t *statePtr, const void *input, size_t length)\nL176: \nL177: Consumes a block of input to an XXH3_state_t.\nL178: \nL179: Definition xxhash.h:6833\nL180: \nL181: cite12†XXH3_64bits_reset L182: \nL183: XXH_errorcode XXH3_64bits_reset(XXH3_state_t *statePtr)\nL184: Resets an XXH3_state_t to begin a new hash.\nL185: \nL186: Definition xxhash.h:6637\nL187: \nL188: cite19†XXH3_freeState L189: \nL190: XXH_errorcode XXH3_freeState(XXH3_state_t *statePtr)\nL191: \nL192: Frees an XXH3_state_t.\nL193: \nL194: Definition xxhash.h:6595\nL195: \nL196: cite14†XXH64_hash_t L197: \nL198: uint64_t XXH64_hash_t\nL199: \nL200: An unsigned 64-bit integer.\nL201: \nL202: Definition xxhash.h:858\nL203: \nL204: cite15†XXH3_state_s L205: \nL206: Definition xxhash.h:1765\nL207: Streaming functions generate the xxHash value from an incremental input. This method is slower than single-call functions, due to state management. For small inputs, prefer `cite6†XXH32() ` and `cite5†XXH64() `, which are better optimized.\nL208: \nL209: An XXH state must first be allocated using `XXH*_createState()`.\nL210: \nL211: Start a new hash by initializing the state with a seed using `XXH*_reset()`.\nL212: \nL213: Then, feed the hash state by calling `XXH*_update()` as many times as necessary.\nL214: The function returns an error code, with 0 meaning OK, and any other value meaning there is an error.\nL215: \nL216: Finally, a hash value can be produced anytime, by using `XXH*_digest()`. This function returns the nn-bits hash as an int or long long.\nL217: \nL218: It's still possible to continue inserting input into the hash state after a digest, and generate new hash values later on by invoking `XXH*_digest()`.\nL219: \nL220: When done, release the state using `XXH*_freeState()`.\nL221: \nL222: Canonical Representation\nL223: The default return values from XXH functions are unsigned 32, 64 and 128 bit integers. This the simplest and fastest format for further post-processing.\nL224: \nL225: However, this leaves open the question of what is the order on the byte level, since little and big endian conventions will store the same number differently.\nL226: \nL227: The canonical representation settles this issue by mandating big-endian convention, the same convention as human-readable numbers (large digits first).\nL228: When writing hash values to storage, sending them over a network, or printing them, it's highly recommended to use the canonical representation to ensure portability across a wider range of systems, present and future.\nL229: \nL230: The following functions allow transformation of hash values to and from canonical format.\nL231: \nL232: cite20†XXH32_canonicalFromHash() , cite21†XXH32_hashFromCanonical() , cite22†XXH64_canonicalFromHash() , cite23†XXH64_hashFromCanonical() , cite24†XXH128_canonicalFromHash() , cite25†XXH128_hashFromCanonical() ,\nL233: #include <stdio.h>\nL234: #include \"cite8†xxhash.h \"\nL235: \nL236: // Example for a function which prints XXH32_hash_t in human readable format\nL237: \nL238: void printXxh32(cite9†XXH32_hash_t hash)\nL239: \nL240: {\nL241: \nL242: cite26†XXH32_canonical_t cano;\nL243: \nL244: cite20†XXH32_canonicalFromHash (&cano, hash);\nL245: \nL246: size_t i;\nL247: \nL248: for(i = 0; i < sizeof(cano.cite27†digest ); ++i) {\nL249: \nL250: printf(\"%02x\", cano.cite27†digest [i]);\nL251: \nL252: }\nL253: \nL254: printf(\"\\n\");\nL255: \nL256: }\nL257: \nL258: // Example for a function which converts XXH32_canonical_t to XXH32_hash_t\nL259: \nL260: cite9†XXH32_hash_t convertCanonicalToXxh32(cite26†XXH32_canonical_t cano)\nL261: \nL262: {\nL263: cite9†XXH32_hash_t hash = cite21†XXH32_hashFromCanonical (&cano);\nL264: \nL265: return hash;\nL266: \nL267: }\nL268: \nL269: cite20†XXH32_canonicalFromHash L270: \nL271: void XXH32_canonicalFromHash(XXH32_canonical_t *dst, XXH32_hash_t hash)\nL272: \nL273: Converts an XXH32_hash_t to a big endian XXH32_canonical_t.\nL274: \nL275: Definition xxhash.h:3324\nL276: \nL277: cite21†XXH32_hashFromCanonical L278: \nL279: XXH32_hash_t XXH32_hashFromCanonical(const XXH32_canonical_t *src)\nL280: \nL281: Converts an XXH32_canonical_t to a native XXH32_hash_t.\nL282: \nL283: Definition xxhash.h:3331\nL284: \nL285: cite26†XXH32_canonical_t L286: Canonical (big endian) representation of XXH32_hash_t.\nL287: \nL288: Definition xxhash.h:755\nL289: \nL290: cite27†XXH32_canonical_t::digest L291: \nL292: unsigned char digest[4]\nL293: \nL294: Definition xxhash.h:756\nL295: \nL296:   * cite28†Generated by 1.11.0\n"
